# gdb setup for the Project 9 debugging lab.
#
# THIS RUNS ON THE HOST. Use it from the directory holding vmlinux:
#
#     gdb-multiarch -x projects/09-kernel-debug/host/gdbinit vmlinux
#
# or, if vmlinux is elsewhere, edit nothing and pass it on the command
# line; this file never names a path to it on purpose, because the one
# thing that must not be guessed here is which vmlinux you meant.
#
# THE RULE THIS FILE EXISTS TO ENFORCE
#
# vmlinux must come from the build that produced the running kernel. Not
# the same source, not the same version: the same build. A vmlinux from a
# different build of identical source resolves addresses to the wrong
# symbols confidently and silently, and the backtrace looks right. That
# failure has cost more debugging time than any bug it was used on.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# The kernel's own gdb helpers: lx-symbols, lx-dmesg, lx-ps and the rest.
# They are generated into the kernel build directory by CONFIG_GDB_SCRIPTS
# as vmlinux-gdb.py next to vmlinux, so this line picks them up only if
# you started gdb where that file is.
#
# If it is not found, gdb prints a warning and carries on without the lx-
# commands, which is survivable but means no lx-symbols and therefore no
# module symbols. debug.cfg sets CONFIG_GDB_SCRIPTS for this reason.
add-auto-load-safe-path .
source ./vmlinux-gdb.py

# Do not page. A backtrace that stops halfway waiting for a keypress is
# a backtrace that scrolls off when you press it.
set pagination off
set confirm off

# The kernel is not a program gdb started, and several of its defaults
# are wrong for it.
set print pretty on
set print array-indexes on

# kgdb speaks the remote protocol over a serial line that is slow and, in
# polled mode, occasionally slower. The default 2 s timeout gives up on a
# board that is merely busy.
set remotetimeout 30

# A convenience for the one command in this project that is always typed
# the same way. The port is agent-proxy's gdb port; see host/agent-proxy.sh.
define kgdb
  target remote localhost:5551
end
document kgdb
Attach to the board through agent-proxy's gdb port (5551).

The board must already be in the debugger. Put it there from the console
session with:

    echo g > /proc/sysrq-trigger

or, over the serial terminal, the SysRq break followed by "g". The
heartbeat LED on GPIO17 freezes when the kernel has stopped, which is how
you know it worked before gdb says anything.
end

# The module's symbols are not in vmlinux. lx-symbols loads them, and it
# needs the directory holding buggy.ko. Run it AFTER attaching, because
# it reads the module list out of the stopped kernel.
define lxmod
  lx-symbols .
end
document lxmod
Load module symbols from the current directory, after attaching.

Run this once connected. Until it has run, a backtrace through the faulty
module shows raw addresses with no names, which looks like a corrupted
stack and is not one.
end

# What the notebook does, in the order it does it. Printed on load so the
# sequence does not have to be remembered at the moment it is needed.
echo \n
echo Project 9 gdb setup loaded.\n
echo \n
echo   kgdb             attach through agent-proxy on port 5551\n
echo   lxmod            load buggy.ko symbols (after attaching)\n
echo   break fault_null set the breakpoint the notebook uses\n
echo   bt / info registers / print victim\n
echo \n
echo Check first that this vmlinux is from the build now running on the\n
echo board. Nothing here can verify that, and a mismatch is silent.\n
echo \n
